        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Young Turks</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and the Third Army were its base; exile circles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">Secret societies</a> of students and cadets after 1889; exile, schisms and repression in the 1890s; the opposition congresses of 1902 and 1907 and the Ottoman Freedom Society (1906); the Young Turk Revolution (1908); the Second Constitutional Era and party rivalry (1908–1913); one-party rule of the CUP and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> (1913–1918).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ibrahim Temo and Abdullah Cevdet, medical students who founded the first society; Ahmed Rıza, the positivist leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; Prince Sabahaddin, champion of decentralization; Mehmed Talaat, founder of the Ottoman Freedom Society; Ahmed Niyazi Bey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a>, leaders of the 1908 revolution; Ziya Gökalp, Turkish nationalist sociologist.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The constitution restored and the Second Constitutional Era opened in 1908, with multi-party politics for the first time; then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> from 1913, entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of Ottoman Christians of 1915 to 1917, masterminded within the CUP; its leaders fled into exile in 1918.</span></div>
        </div>
