    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Young Turks were a diverse network of exiles, students, officers and intellectuals, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, united by opposition to Abdul Hamid II and the wish to restore the constitution of 1876.</li><li>Their Committee of Union and Progress forced the sultan to restore the constitution on 24 July 1908, opening the Second Constitutional Era and the country’s first multi-party democracy.</li><li>After the 1913 Raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a> the CUP ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, took the empire into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and masterminded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of Ottoman Christians from 1915 to 1917.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Committee of Union and Progress owes its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte">Auguste Comte</a>’s positivist motto “Order and Progress”: in 1894 Ahmed Rıza asked the society to call itself Order and Progress, and its members compromised on Union and Progress.</p></aside>
    </section>

