    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1889</span><span class="rf-text">Medical students including Ibrahim Temo and Abdullah Cevdet form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a>, the beginning of the Committee of Union and Progress</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1902</span><span class="rf-text">The First Congress of Ottoman Opposition, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, splits over whether to invite foreign intervention</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1907</span><span class="rf-text">The Second Congress of Ottoman Opposition agrees that Abdul Hamid II must be deposed, without foreign interference</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1908</span><span class="rf-text">Unionist officers of the Third Army mutiny and the sultan restores the constitution, opening the Second Constitutional Era</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1913</span><span class="rf-text">After the Raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a> a radicalized CUP takes power and bans all other parties</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1914</span><span class="rf-text">The CUP takes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on the side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915–1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and other Ottoman Christians, masterminded within the CUP</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1918</span><span class="rf-text">The CUP ministry resigns, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> is signed, and Enver, Talaat and Cemal flee into exile</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The name comes from the French Jeunes Turcs, a label foreign observers gave to many Ottoman reformers of the 19th century. The historian Roderic Davison found no consistent use of it: a Young Turk party was spoken of wherever an amorphous “Old Turk” faction was being confronted. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-ottomans">Young Ottomans</a>, the liberal and Islamist opposition to the government of Fuad and Aali Pasha, were also called Jeunes Turcs, though they called themselves the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>The movement known to history as the Young Turks was the opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdul Hamid II that surfaced after 1889. Abdul Hamid had worked with the Young Ottomans to promulgate the constitution of 1876, written by the reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midhat-pasha">Midhat Pasha</a>, but by 1878 he had dissolved the parliament and closed the First Constitutional Era after only two years. He returned to absolutism, silencing dissent through an extensive secret police, and his reign was marked by massacres of minorities.</p><p>Driven underground with all political dissent, his opponents gathered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> of “progressive medical university students and military cadets”, inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/young-italy">Young Italy</a> movement. By the 1890s they were mainly a loose and contentious network of exiled intellectuals who made a living by selling their newspapers to secret subscribers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/positivism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/positivism.jpg" alt="Positivism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/positivism">Positivism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auguste_Comte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johan Hendrik Hoffmeister, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By and large the Young Turks wanted to take power away from Yıldız Palace and restore constitutional government: the Ottoman Parliament and the constitution of 1876. They believed that a social contract in the form of a constitution would solve the empire’s problems with nationalist movements and foreign intervention by instilling Ottomanism, a multicultural Ottoman nationalism.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/positivism">Positivism</a>, with its claim to be a religion of science, deeply impressed them; they thought it easier to reconcile with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> than popular materialist theories, and it served as a basis for the strong government they wanted. A guiding principle was a society in which religion played no consequential role, but since this was hard to spread among a deeply religious peasantry and elite, some argued that Islam itself was materialistic. The ulema accused them of “trying to change Islam into another form and create a new religion while calling it Islam”.</p><p>The movement held a mosaic of ideas, with three main currents for the state: multicultural Ottomanism, pan-Islamism and pan-Turkism. Ahmed Rıza wanted a centralized, Turkish-dominated empire free of European influence; Prince Sabahaddin a decentralized, federal state built on capitalism and liberalism. After the congress of 1902 Turkish nationalism gained ground, and Rıza replaced the word “Ottoman” with “Turk”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balkan-wars.jpg" alt="Balkan Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luleburgaz_Battle_Postcard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1894 Ahmed Rıza joined the Committee of Ottoman Union, which took the name Union and Progress and was organized around his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> paper Meşveret. In August 1896 a coup by cabinet ministers aligned with the CUP was leaked to the palace; a plot by Unionist cadets the next year brought the largest crackdown of the Hamidian era, with more than 630 arrests and exiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> deported Rıza in 1896, and after the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1897 the sultan won many exiles back with amnesties and buy-outs.</p><p>The First Congress of Ottoman Opposition, in Paris on 4 February 1902, split over foreign intervention: Sabahaddin’s followers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were for it, Rıza’s Unionists against. In 1906 Mehmed Talaat founded the Ottoman Freedom Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>, which recruited officers of the Third Army and came to control the CUP. The Second Congress, in Paris on 22 December 1907, united the opposition behind deposing Abdul Hamid by any means necessary, without foreign interference. By June 1908 Unionist officers of the Third Army had mutinied, and on 24 July the sultan restored the constitution.</p><p>An attempted countercoup, the 31 March Incident of April 1909, ended in Abdul Hamid’s deposition. After the 1912 coup, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913 and the 1913 Raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a>, a radicalized CUP took over. Through secret negotiations it allied the empire with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 2 November 1914 the empire entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enver-pasha.jpg" alt="Enver Pasha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enver_Pasha_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicola Perscheid, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first organisers were the medical students Ibrahim Temo and Abdullah Cevdet, a Kurdish intellectual who supported biological materialism and secularism; their society grew to include exiles, civil servants and army officers. Ahmed Rıza, a positivist based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, pressed a Turkish nationalist and secularist agenda but denounced revolution, facing both the conservative and Islamist Mehmet Murat Bey and the CUP’s “Activist” faction. Prince Sabahaddin, of the Ottoman dynasty, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1899 and with Ismail Kemal founded the Ottoman Freedom Lovers’ Committee and later the Private Enterprise and Decentralization League.</p><p>Despite the name, members included many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Among them were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> journalist Yusuf Akçura, who opposed Ottomanism and supported the separation of church and state; Mehmet Cavit Bey, minister of finance, hanged for treason in 1926; Marcel Samuel Raphael Cohen (Tekin Alp), an ideologue of pan-Turkism; and the sociologist Ziya Gökalp, a Turkish nationalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a>.</p><p>Officers made the revolution of 1908: Ahmed Niyazi Bey was its initiator and a leader, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a> and Eyüp Sabri. Talaat Pasha, Enver Pasha and Bahaeddin Şakir were principal among those within the CUP who masterminded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of 1915 to 1917. Opposition also ran through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> lodges and Masonic lodges.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The revolution of 1908 achieved the movement’s first aim, bringing multi-party democracy for the first time in the country’s history. The Young Turks then formalized their differences: the liberal, decentralist wing formed the Liberty Party and later the Freedom and Accord Party, while the Turkish nationalist, centralist wing stayed in the CUP. In power the CUP carried out secularizing and centralizing reforms but was criticized for a pro-Turkish ideology, and many non-Turkish and non-Muslim Young Turks joined their own national movements.</p><p>At its first open congress, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> in 1911, the CUP proclaimed policies of disarming Christians, barring them from buying property, settling Muslims in Christian territories and Ottomanizing all subjects by persuasion or by force of arms. By 1913 it had banned all other parties; parliament became a rubber stamp, and policy was made in the Central Committee.</p><p>Its rule ended in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Scholars trace the Armenian genocide to long-standing discrimination and land conflicts in the Ottoman east, radicalized by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, when CUP leaders embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkification">Turkification</a> and came to see the Christians of Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> as a threat. In the war, Young Turk leaders branded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> collectively guilty and ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> and annihilation. Up to 1,200,000 Armenians were deported and about half of them died; the Sayfo, the genocide of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, ran alongside it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk.jpg" alt="Mustafa Kemal Atatürk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ataturk1930s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defeat ended the CUP’s rule: its ministry resigned on 13 October 1918, and on 2 November Enver, Talaat and Cemal fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The Freedom and Accord Party purged the Unionists, and the empire soon collapsed. In 1926, after an assassination attempt by remaining Unionists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> was quoted denouncing the “Young Turk Party”, men “who should have been made to account for the millions of our Christian subjects who were ruthlessly driven en masse from their homes and massacred”.</p><p>In 2005 the International Association of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">Genocide</a> Scholars stated that the CUP government had begun “a systematic genocide of its Armenian citizens”, in which more than a million were exterminated. The historian Uğur Ümit Üngör has argued that the Republican People’s Party, founded by Mustafa Kemal, was the CUP’s successor and continued its ethnic cleansing in Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> until 1950. Talaat Pasha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha">Cemal Pasha</a> were assassinated in exile during Operation Nemesis, an Armenian revenge operation: Soghomon Tehlirian, whose family had been killed in the genocide, shot Talaat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and was acquitted by a German jury. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a> was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> near Baldzhuan.</p><p>The CUP’s drive for centralization continued an effort begun with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms. Today “Young Turk” means an insurgent impatiently pressing for reform within an organization, and groups in many countries have been called Young Turks for their rebellious or revolutionary nature.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Young Turks were the opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdul Hamid II’s absolutism, a diverse network of exiles, students and officers whose Committee of Union and Progress forced the restoration of the constitution in 1908. The CUP went on to rule as a one-party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, took the empire into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and masterminded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of Ottoman Christians from 1915 to 1917.</em></p>

